{"reference":"Jeremiah 18","book":"Jeremiah","chapter":18,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Questions Raised by Conditional Judgment","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Verses 7–10 describe announced judgment and promised good as conditional on a nation's response. Jonah 3:10 offers a related example: Nineveh turns from evil, and the announced disaster is not carried out. This connection helps clarify that Jeremiah's call to turn in verse 11 is a real invitation, while traditions differ over how to relate such divine relenting to foreknowledge and immutability.","verse_refs":["Jeremiah 18:7-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_jeremiah_018_534603c56de76d9c1af06185"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Why the Conditional Language Matters","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The potter image is followed by a direct statement about nations: destructive and beneficial announcements are conditioned by their conduct. Read with the example of Nineveh's turning, this prevents verses 7–11 from being treated as an unqualified declaration of a fixed outcome; the passage itself makes repentance and continued evil decisive within its warning.","verse_refs":["Jeremiah 18:7-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_jeremiah_018_71a475149df3c79980519b17"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Jeremiah's Prayer Under Threat","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"After opponents plot against Jeremiah and dismiss his words, verses 19–23 become a first-person prayer of protest and petition. This passage is commonly associated with Jeremiah's “confessions,” a label for laments that portray accusation, isolation, protest, compulsion, petition, and despair. The exact boundaries of this group and how directly it reflects Jeremiah's biography remain disputed, but the category highlights the sharp shift from public warning to the prophet's personal appeal.","verse_refs":["Jeremiah 18:18-23"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_jeremiah_018_75619255eacf91dab3059590"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Book Around This Chapter","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Jeremiah 18 belongs to a book that combines poetry, prose sermons, narratives, letters, headings, repeated accounts, restoration collections, oracles concerning nations, and an appendix. A practical outline places it within chapters 2–25, while the book's long compositional and textual history remains disputed. That larger shape helps readers expect shifts in form and voice, including the movement in this chapter from symbolic action and public address to personal lament.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_jeremiah_018_4e413c30828617560a29f9a6"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}